     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this one, even with your confirmation. 5,100 mg/day of metformin is twice the maximum labeled dose, and I don't know of any indication that supports it. The extra dose adds a real risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity, with no added glucose-lowering benefit. The benefit plateaus around 2,000 mg/day.\n\nHere is what I can do right now:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** For example, 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg/day), or 1000 mg twice daily (2,000 mg/day) with meals.\n- **Enter a titration plan** if you're trying to reach a target dose. For example, start at 500 mg with meals and increase gradually.\n\nIf you think this patient needs a dose above the labeled maximum, please talk with the pharmacist or attending first. That order would need to go through the pharmacy review or override pathway, not through me.\n\nTell me which of the options above you want, and I'll enter it."
      }
